Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy
of this software and associated documentation files (the "Software"), to deal
in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights
to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell
copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is
furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions.

2. ATTRIBUTION CONDITION (MANDATORY)

The above copyright notice, this license text, and a clear reference to the
original source (https://github.com/Jeesson/vibe-ui) MUST be retained in
all copies or substantial portions of the Software, including in source code,
"About"/credits sections of derived applications, package metadata, and
distributed documentation. Removal or obfuscation of the attribution to the
original source terminates this license.

3. REVENUE SHARING CONDITION (MANDATORY)

Any direct revenue derived from the Software or derivatives thereof —
including but not limited to sponsorships, donations, paid subscriptions,
commercial support, and integration fees — MUST be shared as follows:

  - 50% (fifty percent) to the rights holders of the original source
    (https://github.com/Jeesson/vibe-ui);
  - 50% (fifty percent) to the authors of the derived distribution.

Failure to comply with this revenue sharing condition terminates all rights
granted under this license.

3.1. BREACH ESCALATION (MANDATORY REMEDY)

If the licensee breaches any condition of this license — including, without
limitation, failure to attribute the original source, misrepresentation of
origin, removal or obfuscation of attribution, late or incomplete revenue
payments, or any attempt to circumvent the revenue sharing obligations — the
revenue sharing ratio defined in Section 3 is immediately and automatically
revised in favor of the rights holders of the original source as follows:

  - 80% (eighty percent) to the rights holders of the original source
    (https://github.com/Jeesson/vibe-ui), including all amounts accrued
    from the beginning of the infringing activity;
  - 20% (twenty percent) to the breaching party.

The revised 80/20 ratio applies retroactively (with obligation to pay back
the shortfall) and remains in effect permanently, unless the rights holders
of the original source explicitly restore the original 50/50 ratio in
writing. This escalation is without prejudice to any other legal remedies
available to the rights holders of the original source.

3.2. CIRCUMVENTION

Any scheme designed to conceal, understate, or divert revenue — including
routing income through third parties, off-book arrangements, or barter
deals — is deemed a breach under Section 3.1. In case of dispute, the
rights holders of the original source may require the breaching party to
provide accounting records sufficient to verify the revenue amounts; refusal
to provide such records is itself deemed a breach under Section 3.1.

3.3. PAYMENT TERMS AND DEFAULT INTEREST

Revenue shares are payable within thirty (30) days after the end of each
calendar quarter unless otherwise agreed in writing. Overdue amounts bear
default interest of one percent (1%) per month (or the maximum rate allowed
by law, whichever is higher), accruing daily. Payment costs, currency
conversion fees, and taxes (except income taxes of the payee) are borne by
the paying party. Revenue received in cryptocurrency or foreign currency is
valued at the exchange rate on the date of receipt; favorable or unfavorable
subsequent rate movements do not affect the obligation.

3.4. FUTURE UPDATES OF THE LICENSE

The rights holders of the original source may publish updated versions of
this license ("New Versions"). Derivatives distributed under this license
automatically become subject to the terms of the latest New Version published
by the rights holders of the original source, unless the licensee obtains a
separate signed commercial agreement. Continuing to use, copy, or distribute
the Software or derivatives after the publication of a New Version constitutes
acceptance of its terms.

3.5. BINDING ON SUCCESSORS AND FORKS

The obligations under Sections 2, 3, 3.1–3.4 bind not only the original
licensee but also any person or entity that receives, forks, re-forks,
redistributes, or otherwise acquires the Software or derivatives from the
licensee, directly or through intermediaries. Licensees are jointly and
severally liable for breaches committed by parties they distributed the
Software to, to the extent of the revenue derived from that distribution.

3.6. COSTS, FEES, AND VENUE

In any dispute related to this license, the breaching party reimburses the
rights holders of the original source all reasonable costs of enforcement,
including attorney fees, expert and audit costs, and translation fees,
regardless of whether litigation was initiated. The rights holders of the
original source may also publish a factual notice of the breach (including
the project name, URL, and nature of the breach) in the original repository
and related channels; such notice does not constitute defamation.

3.7. NO WAIVER

Any delay or omission by the rights holders of the original source in
exercising their rights under this license (including acceptance of late or
partial payments, or continued interaction with the licensee) does not
constitute a waiver of those rights or of the escalation remedy in
Section 3.1. The 50/50 ratio applies only while all conditions are
continuously observed; it is never "grandfathered" by prior practice.

3.8. AUDIT PREMIUM

The rights holders of the original source may, at reasonable intervals and
with no fewer than fourteen (14) days' written notice, request revenue
records sufficient to verify the declared revenue shares. If an independent
review reveals that the declared revenue understates actual revenue by more
than ten percent (10%), the licensee shall pay (a) the full reasonable cost
of the review, and (b) the underpaid revenue share amount multiplied by two
(2). This remedy is cumulative with, not instead of, Section 3.1.

3.9. REVENUE SNAPSHOT

For the purpose of calculating revenue shares, the reference base for each
payment period is the highest-grossing quarter of the preceding twelve (12)
months, regardless of the actual revenue of the current period. Artificial
or structural reduction of revenue in the current period (including
suspension of monetization, transfer of income streams to related projects,
or voluntary discontinuation of paid offerings) does not reduce the
obligation.

3.10. DERIVATIVES REGISTRY

The licensee shall maintain and submit to the rights holders of the original
source, at least quarterly (via pull request, issue, or the contact channel
designated by the rights holders), an up-to-date list of all public
deployments, distributions, and forks of the Software or derivatives thereof,
including names, URLs, and versions. Failure to submit the list on time, or
submission of an incomplete or materially inaccurate list, constitutes a
breach under Section 3.1.

3.11. GOVERNING LAW AND FORUM

Any dispute arising out of or in connection with this license shall be
resolved exclusively in the courts or arbitral forum of the place of
residence or registered office of the rights holders of the original source,
under the substantive law of the rights holders' jurisdiction, in the
language of the rights holders. The breaching party irrevocably submits to
this forum and waives any objection based on inconvenient forum.

3.12. AUTOMATIC TRANSFER UPON CESSATION

Upon bankruptcy, liquidation, cessation of business activity, or voluntary
abandonment (no meaningful activity for twelve (12) consecutive months) of
the licensee, all of the licensee's rights in and to its derivatives of the
Software automatically transfer to the rights holders of the original
source, without further action, consideration, or consent. The rights
holders may rely on this Section when notifying hosting platforms or
package registries of the termination of the derivative's license.

3.13. SECURITY CLOSE-OUT

If a security vulnerability of high or critical severity is discovered in a
distributed derivative, the rights holders of the original source may notify
the licensee; the licensee shall release a fix within thirty (30) days of
notice. Failure to do so constitutes a breach terminating the license as to
the derivative, whereupon the rights holders may (a) publicly designate the
derivative as unsupported and unsafe, and (b) request takedown of the
derivative's repository, package, or distribution from hosting platforms and
package registries.

3.14. PROHIBITION OF SECOND-LEVEL DERIVATIVES ("FORKS OF FORKS")

A "Sub-Fork" is any derivative, modification, fork, re-fork, or
re-publication of the Software created by any person or entity other than
the first-level licensee — i.e., received from the first-level licensee,
directly or through intermediaries.

3.14.1. SUB-FORKS ARE PROHIBITED. The creation, publication,
redistribution, sublicensing, or monetization of any Sub-Fork is strictly
prohibited and constitutes an unlicensed infringement from the moment of
creation. Only the first-level licensee may hold rights to a derivative;
no party below the first-level licensee may create, hold, or distribute a
derivative of any kind without a separate written agreement with the rights
holders of the original source.

3.14.2. NO REVENUE FROM SUB-FORKS. One hundred percent (100%) of any
revenue derived from a Sub-Fork — including sponsorships, donations, paid
subscriptions, commercial support, and integration fees — belongs
exclusively to the rights holders of the original source
(https://github.com/Jeesson/vibe-ui), regardless of the creator's
intent or belief that the activity was licensed. This entitlement is
retroactive to the beginning of the infringing activity.

3.14.3. RECIPIENTS RECEIVE USE ONLY. The first-level licensee may grant
recipients of the Software only a right of use (internal, non-distributable
use); recipients do not acquire any right to modify-and-republish, fork,
redistribute, or monetize the Software. Distribution of the Software to a
third party by anyone other than the first-level licensee is deemed
creation of a prohibited Sub-Fork under Section 3.14.1.

3.14.4. CHAIN LIABILITY. The first-level licensee must ensure that every
recipient receives this license in full (including this Section 3.14) and
remains jointly and severally liable for violations of this Section by the
parties it distributed the Software to, in accordance with Section 3.5.

3.14.5. ATTRIBUTION STILL MANDATORY. The attribution condition of
Section 2 applies to all recipients without exception; any copy lacking
full attribution to the original source is unlicensed from the moment of
its creation.

